22
00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:12.200
Part 1 of nine: Start with the person and the

23
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:12.460
purpose.

24
00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:16.150
The standards connected with this part are 1.

25
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:20.340
Here is the work this part helps you prepare: Choose

26
00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.980
a work example and describe the intended experience

27
00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:28.310
In the curriculum's reference index, standard 1 is described as:

28
00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:31.850
Understandable, respectful services responsive to the person.

29
00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:34.480
Welcome to your learning.

30
00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:38.670
Before we open the standards, what can someone expect to

31
00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:39.330
do in this learning?

32
00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:44.000
Welcome. This learning is about making the work you already

33
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.880
do more understandable, accessible and responsive to people. You do

34
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.560
not need to know every culture or have authority over

35
00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.120
an entire program to begin. You do need a willingness

36
00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.120
to notice, ask, and follow through.

37
00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:58.690
Across these lessons, we will use the National CLAS Standards

38
00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:02.630
to examine practical decisions. The name refers to culturally and

39
00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:06.190
linguistically appropriate services. The standards come from the United States

40
00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:08.910
Department of Health and Human Services Office of Minority Health.

41
00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:12.390
They provide a framework for organizations. Here, we will connect

42
00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:15.850
that framework to individual work within the Disability Services Division.

43
00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:21.340
This is voluntary, self-directed learning. You can pause, revisit an

44
00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:24.020
explanation, or take a break. You do not need a

45
00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:26.440
partner or a group. When you reach a reflection, you

46
00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:28.900
can think quietly, use your own notes, or work with

47
00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:30.940
the companion sheet. You do not need to share personal

48
00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:31.560
experiences.

49
00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:36.460
Please leave names, case details, health information and other private

50
00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:38.060
information out of your learning notes.

51
00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:40.350
Choose something you can work with.

52
00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.260
Let's make it concrete. What is a useful work example

53
00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:45.920
to carry through all nine parts?

54
00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:49.950
Choose one ordinary part of your work that you can

55
00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:53.170
examine safely. It might be a public notice, an instruction,

56
00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:57.470
a form, a meeting invitation, an internal process, or a

57
00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:00.130
recommendation you help prepare. If you do not have an

58
00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:02.730
example in mind, follow the sample service notice that we

59
00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:03.970
will use throughout this curriculum.

60
00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:08.240
Our sample notice asks a person to attend a service-planning

61
00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:12.380
conversation. It explains the date but uses unfamiliar acronyms. It

62
00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:15.100
provides a telephone number without explaining how to request communication

63
00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:18.500
assistance. Staff have noticed that some people do not respond.

64
00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:22.320
Those observations give us questions to explore. They do not

65
00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.840
tell us why any particular person did not respond.

66
00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:29.230
For your own example, write one sentence about its purpose.

67
00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:32.770
Then describe what the person receiving it needs to be

68
00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:36.350
able to do. Those are different questions. The organization's purpose

69
00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:37.910
may be to schedule a meeting.

70
00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:41.510
The person's task may be to understand why the meeting

71
00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:46.270
matters, consider their choices, arrange the support they want, and

72
00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:47.690
respond in a way they can use.

73
00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:51.080
Pause here if you would like to choose your example.

74
00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:52.280
You can change it later.

75
00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:54.940
The principal standard.

76
00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:59.200
When we say the person's experience comes first, what does

77
00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:00.860
standard one ask us to notice?

78
00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:05.870
Standard one is the foundation. In practical terms, it asks

79
00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:09.590
us to provide quality services that people can understand, that

80
00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:13.010
respect them, and that respond to their cultural beliefs, language,

81
00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:17.510
health literacy and other communication needs. This explanation is a

82
00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:20.550
guide to application. The original wording is linked from the

83
00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:21.510
companion lesson page.

84
00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.580
Notice that the focus is the person's experience of the

85
00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:29.240
service, not simply whether we sent information. A letter can

86
00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:32.360
be accurate and still be difficult to use. An interpreter

87
00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:35.260
can be scheduled and still arrive too late for meaningful

88
00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:35.820
participation.

89
00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:39.560
A form can be online and still be inaccessible to

90
00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:42.400
someone who uses assistive technology or does not have reliable

91
00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:43.320
internet access.

92
00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:47.410
Equity asks us to consider what people need to participate

93
00:04:47.410 --> 00:04:50.690
meaningfully. Giving everyone the same format does not tell us

94
00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:53.150
whether everyone can use it. At the same time, we

95
00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:56.330
should not assume a person's needs from their name, appearance,

96
00:04:56.610 --> 00:05:01.210
disability, language or community membership. Ask respectfully, offer choices within

97
00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:04.210
the available process, and confirm whether the response works.

98
00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:06.700
How the standards connect.

99
00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:10.990
How does one person's work connect with the fifteen standards

100
00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:11.650
as a whole?

101
00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:16.660
There are fifteen standards. Standard one states the central purpose.

102
00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:21.380
Standards two through four address governance, leadership and workforce. Standards

103
00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:26.000
five through eight address communication and language assistance. Standards nine

104
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:29.340
through fifteen address engagement, improvement and accountability.

105
00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:34.690
These are connected responsibilities. A staff member may recognize that

106
00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:37.890
a notice needs a clear offer of language assistance. Making

107
00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:41.790
that offer dependable may also require an approved process, trained

108
00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:45.270
staff, resources, community input and a way to monitor whether

109
00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:48.310
assistance actually reaches people. That is why we will move

110
00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:51.790
between everyday interactions and the organizational conditions that support them.

111
00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:56.620
You are not expected to personally perform every organizational responsibility.

112
00:05:56.620 --> 00:05:59.640
Part of good practice is recognizing the difference between a

113
00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:02.960
change within your authority and an issue that needs coordination

114
00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:05.960
or approval. We will return to that distinction throughout the

115
00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:06.200
curriculum.

116
00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:07.970
Try a first decision.

117
00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:12.060
Back to the notice: what would help us understand why

118
00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:13.180
people aren't responding?

119
00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:17.550
Consider the sample notice. The team wants to improve response

120
00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:20.710
rates. One suggestion is to send the same notice more

121
00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:23.790
often. Another is to ask how people experience the notice

122
00:06:23.790 --> 00:06:26.810
and examine whether its instructions and assistance options are usable.

123
00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:29.690
Which approach gives you a better starting point for understanding

124
00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:30.290
the barrier?

125
00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:35.920
Pause and make your choice in the companion activity. The

126
00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:39.580
feedback will explain the reasoning. You can revise your answer;

127
00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:40.740
the purpose is to practice.

128
00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:45.290
Sending a reminder may eventually help, but repeating an unclear

129
00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:48.590
message can repeat the same barrier. Start by understanding the

130
00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:51.790
experience. That does not mean delaying every action until you

131
00:06:51.790 --> 00:06:55.150
have perfect information. It means making a reasonable inquiry, trying

132
00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:56.870
an appropriate change, and checking what happened.

133
00:06:57.600 --> 00:06:59.560
Your first working note.

134
00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:03.050
What should I keep in my working notes before moving

135
00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:03.250
on?

136
00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:06.940
Finish this lesson with three short notes: the work example

137
00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:09.860
you chose, the person or people who need to use

138
00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:12.780
it, and what successful participation would look like from their

139
00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:16.920
perspective. Keep these notes general and free of personal information.

140
00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:20.230
In the next lesson, we will look more closely at

141
00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:24.030
the difference between an observation and an assumption. That distinction

142
00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:27.070
helps us ask better questions and choose a useful response.

143
00:07:27.790 --> 00:07:30.430
For now, remember this starting point: a process is not

144
00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:32.910
fully understood by looking only at what we send or

145
00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:33.370
what we intend.

146
00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:37.030
We also need to learn how people encounter and use

147
00:07:37.030 --> 00:07:37.230
it.

148
00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:42.320
Let's work through the companion example: Our working example: a

149
00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:43.260
service-planning notice

150
00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:47.750
Here is the situation. A notice gives a meeting date,

151
00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:51.570
uses several unfamiliar terms and lists a telephone number without

152
00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:55.370
explaining how to request communication assistance. Some recipients do not

153
00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:55.670
respond.

154
00:07:56.420 --> 00:07:59.280
What response would move this example forward responsibly?

155
00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:04.190
Begin by describing the person's task: understand why the meeting

156
00:08:04.190 --> 00:08:08.090
matters, consider their choices, arrange the support they want and

157
00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:09.430
use a clear response route.

158
00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:13.400
And here is why that response fits. This separates the

159
00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:16.520
organization's task of sending a notice from the person's experience

160
00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:17.380
of using it.

161
00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:22.130
Now make your own choice. Several people have not responded

162
00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:25.290
to a service-planning notice. What is the most useful first

163
00:08:25.290 --> 00:08:25.590
step?

164
00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:30.660
Option 1. Send the same notice more often before examining

165
00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:31.220
the process.

166
00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:36.130
Option 2. Examine the notice and response routes, and seek

167
00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:38.010
appropriate information about people's experience.

168
00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:42.980
Option 3. Conclude that people who do not respond are

169
00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:43.500
not interested.

170
00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:46.990
Pause the player now if you want to choose and

171
00:08:46.990 --> 00:08:49.830
explain your reasoning. You can use your own notes or

172
00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:53.350
the companion activity. When you're ready, resume and we'll work

173
00:08:53.350 --> 00:08:54.610
through all three responses.

174
00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:57.280
How should we think about option 1?

175
00:08:57.910 --> 00:09:01.450
A reminder may help some people, but repeating the same

176
00:09:01.450 --> 00:09:05.250
message can repeat the same barrier. First examine how people

177
00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:07.570
receive, understand and use the notice.

178
00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:10.660
How should we think about option 2?

179
00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:15.350
This begins with inquiry. Check the purpose, next step, assistance

180
00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:19.070
offer and available feedback without assuming why any individual has

181
00:09:19.070 --> 00:09:19.690
not responded.

182
00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:24.220
Option 2 is the supported choice in this activity.

183
00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:27.230
How should we think about option 3?

184
00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:33.280
A response pattern does not establish motivation. Language, format, timing,

185
00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:35.860
delivery and other conditions may affect participation.

186
00:09:36.770 --> 00:09:39.830
You can choose another response and compare the reasoning.

187
00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:44.600
Choose one non-sensitive work example. What is its purpose, who

188
00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:47.520
needs to use it, and what would successful participation look

189
00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:47.720
like?

190
00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:52.070
The working note from this part is: Work example and

191
00:09:52.070 --> 00:09:52.990
intended experience

192
00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:56.220
Pause the player if you want time to make that

193
00:09:56.220 --> 00:09:59.940
note. Keep it general, without names or private details. Thinking

194
00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:01.640
it through without writing is also fine.

195
00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:05.770
That completes part 1. You can return to any passage

196
00:10:05.770 --> 00:10:08.550
or take a break. The next part is Notice barriers

197
00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:09.590
and ask without assumptions.
